all right guys this is the video you've  been waiting weeks for  the comparison between our eight core  2025k  imac for 2300 and the 10 core  3300 model and as you saw in the title  we are recommending that most people do  not upgrade  to the 10 core i9 cpu for an extra 400  there are a small amount of people who  should upgrade  and i'll mention that in just a few  minutes as we get deeper into our suite  of performance  tests like video editing and final cut  pro 10  programming with xcode music production  with logic pro 10  photo editing with photoshop and  lightroom classic graphics rendering  with blender  gaming performance and more before we  get started i want to show you guys the  full specs of each machine  and one of the major differences is that  the 8 core gets the base  5500 xt graphics card and the 10 core  gets the 5700 xt  with double the vram we also paid 100  for the optional 10 gigabit ethernet  port on the high-end model  so if you don't need that port the price  goes down to 34.50  and by the way those total prices you're  seeing include the ram  that we bought from amazon and upgraded  ourselves for a significant discount  instead of paying apple's incredibly  high ram prices  so we got 32 gigs for the 8 core imac  for 120  and 64 gigs for the 10 core for just 240  off amazon and we'll have links to the  ram that we chose down in the  description below  so you can do the same and if this video  ends up helping you out consider buying  our apple product merch  like our mask or a t-shirt down below to  help support this channel  now let's get into the performance  testing starting with the geekbench 5  benchmark  and in the single core test there was  barely any difference at all  but in the multi-core test the 10 core  i9 model was around nine percent more  powerful  in terms of raw performance now in the  cinematic r20 cpu stress test  which actually factors in the cooling  systems the 10 core model was around 12  percent more powerful which is honestly  less of a difference than i was  expecting  since you're paying 400 for that cpu  upgrade  but the most interesting thing is that  during that test  the 8 core imac was actually able to get  a higher spike  in wattage and then once the temp  stabilized after multiple runs of  cinematic r20  the 10 core was running at a lower clock  speed of 4 gigahertz  because it has to keep those extra cores  cool and this is going to be very  important for some of the real world  tests that we're about to run in just  a minute but first let's go through the  graphics and the gaming tests  in geekbench 5's metal graphics test the  5700 xd  graphics in the higher end model was  about 38  more powerful than the 5500 xt  which is definitely a more impressive  difference than we saw with the 10 core  cpu  and then we ran unigine's heaven gaming  benchmark on the xtreme preset  to test the difference in real world  gaming performance  and to our surprise the higher end model  got around 50 percent more  fps which is an incredible difference  for gamers  and definitely worth the upgrade price  of 500  now as a cherry on top the 5700 xt gets  double the vram 16 gigs instead of only  8 gigs  on the 5500 xt and not only that  but the bandwidth speed is higher as  well 384 gigabytes per second  instead of 224 on the base graphics  which is going to help a lot for some of  the real world graphics tests that i'm  about to show you guys in a minute but  before we get into those video editing  tests with final cut pro 10  i want to cover some other things like  photo editing logic pro 10  and xcode programming we ran the puget  bench photoshop benchmark and we were  surprised to see  that the high end model only scored  around 5  higher even with the 10 core cpu and the  extra ram  so this shows that graphics designers  should be fine with  the lower end model now i tried to run  puget bench for after effects  multiple times but no matter what i did  it wouldn't finish the test i don't know  why  but with that let's get into the  lightroom classic testing  exporting 500 raw 42 megapixel images  and the higher end imac finished exactly  two minutes quicker which isn't much but  it's definitely something now here's the  kicker  in a previous video we actually tested  the differences in how much ram you  should buy  and while using the lower end eight core  imac  we ran the same test with 64 gigs of ram  and it finished in just 22 minutes and  10 seconds  so this not only shows how important ram  is for photo editing in lightroom  but it shows that the a core is actually  faster than the 10 core  if both of them are using 64 gigs of ram  and that's because the 8 core runs at  higher clock speeds  like we saw in the cinebench r20 test  now we also tested building one-to-one  previews  and here the 10 core imac was just four  seconds faster  than the a-core assuming both systems  have  64 gigs of ram now moving on to the max  tech xcode benchmark  which you can download using the link  below in the description  the 10 core imac finished the build only  12 seconds faster  than the a core we personally think this  is due to the fact that on the 10 core  apple is limiting the maximum wattage  and the clock speeds  to help keep the temps down while the a  core feels like it's unlocked  and free to get high spikes in wattage  which has been very  impressive so far and now moving on to  the logic pro 10 benchmark  which tests how many tracks the system  can handle before overloading  the 8 core was able to handle 123 tracks  and the 10 core was able to handle a  slightly higher  131 tracks which is once again  pretty underwhelming and honestly most  logic pro 10 users aren't  maxing out their systems with as many  tracks as possible  so the 8 core should really be fine for  most people  and by the way 64 gigs of ram with the a  core  helped it handle 125 tracks instead of  123.  now before we get started with video  editing we have just one more test to  run  and that's 3d rendering with blenders  bmw and classroom tests  which are actually using the graphics to  render instead of the cpu  in this test the higher end model  finished almost 40 percent faster than  the base model which is a pretty huge  difference  and that performance gain is due to the  5700 xt's  extra raw performance and core count now  let's finally get into video editing  with final cut  and prepare yourself because these  results are insane  in the bruce x 5k benchmark the higher  end model finished the test just  one second faster than the lower end  imac then moving on to our one minute  hvc  stabilization test the difference was  even smaller  with the high-end model finishing just  half a second faster  now the reason for that is because both  systems are using the same  exact quick sync encoders and the same  exact t2 chip  to encode the hevc footage in this test  so the extra raw performance doesn't  really help we then exported a 5 minute  4k  h.264 clip and once again the higher end  model only finished one second faster  because the hardware accelerators are  taking care of the encoding  so it's not limited at all by graphics  power  so because we didn't see a difference in  that test we exported one of our very  own maxtec videos  which is 12 minutes long and comes  packed with a bunch of effects and  charts that we use on a daily basis  and here the high-end model did finish  23 seconds faster  and we think it's because of the much  faster 384 gigabyte per second vram  bandwidth  that is able to more quickly render the  effects now moving on to our five-minute  hevc  export test the high-end model finished  just 12 seconds faster  which is honestly not much of a  difference for paying an extra 500  and of course that's because they both  have the same t2 chip  which takes care of hevc encoding now  what those tests show us is that if  you're doing basic 4k  editing there's really no difference  between the two  but as you start adding more and more  effects like we did in our 12 minute  long max tech video  the higher end 5700 xt model will start  to perform  a bit faster so keep that in mind if  you're going to be adding a lot of  effects  now getting into canon's new eos r5  footage  max did a dedicated video on his channel  for editing r5  and sony a7s3 footage so definitely  check that out after this video is over  but i do have a couple of tests to show  you guys right now in terms of  transcoding a 30 minute 4k  10 bit clip they basically finished in  the same amount of time  with neither of the machines using all  of their cpu power  so for this test the 10 core is  basically pointless  now instead of transcoding we exported a  5 minute version of this footage  and you guys are about to have your  minds blown the 10 core imac  was slower than the 8 core imac by about  8 seconds  and the only reason we could think of is  that the 8 core is running at higher  clock speeds  so if you want to see more info on that  definitely check out max's dedicated  video  now moving on to canon c200 raw footage  final cut pro just got updated to  support metal-based rendering  for this type of footage but it's  currently broken  because canon still hasn't updated their  plugin  so we ended up using the old version of  final cut to run our 5 minute export  test  and here the high-end imac was almost  two minutes  faster but the important thing to note  is that the eight core cpu is still not  the limitation  the extra gains for the higher end imac  are from the better graphics card and as  far as timeline smoothness  the high-end imac was able to play it  back at a perfect  60 frames per second compared to around  54  fps on the low end imac so the graphics  is a great investment here  moving on to redraw footage now using  the new final cut pro version  in red's newly released plug-in we ran  our five-minute 4.5 k export  and the high-end imac was over 25 faster  in this test but not because of the 10  core cpu  mostly because of the faster graphics  yet again  and now exporting our 5-minute ak clip  the high-end imac was  way faster almost 50 faster than  the other imac which is very impressive  and you might be thinking  there you go that's the 10 core cpu  finally shining  but you're wrong max tested before and  after the new final cut pro update  and the high-end imac used to export 4.5  k red raw  with 91 percent of the cpu being used  but now it went down to only 12 percent  of the cpu being used  and for the a core export it went from  100  cpu usage down to 12 cpu usage  with the graphics actually being  basically maxed out  at 98 usage so this basically shows that  in the past  red raw footage would completely max out  your cpu  and make the 10 core way worth it  but now it's mostly graphics based so  the 5700 xt  is very worth it for doing any type of  raw footage editing and as for the 8k  playback test the high-end imac played  it back at 19 fps out of 24  compared to only 12 fps on the lower end  imac  so this crazy ak footage is still being  limited  by the best 5700 xt graphics option  alright guys so with all of those tests  out of the way we can  safely say that the 10 core cpu is  simply  not worth the extra 400 even for the  tasks that are purely processor based  like xcode logic pro 10 photoshop  and lightroom classic which actually got  slower  with the 10 core cpu assuming both  machines have 64 gigs of ram  now i'm not trying to say that the 10  core is bad it's just that this year's 8  core i7  is insane it's incredibly powerful and  it's not being limited  like the 10 core is now as far as all of  the video editing tests  the high-end imac was only faster  because of the better graphics card  and we know this for sure because the 8  cpu  was not being maxed out in any of the  tests even  raw editing which used to max out the  processor  and since the a-core was running at  higher clock speeds  it could actually be even better for  video editing  like we saw in the canon eos r5 footage  test  so with all of that said in the  beginning of this video  i did mention that the 10 core is  actually worth it for a small number of  people  so let me explain if you're the type of  person who has too much money to even  care about getting the best bang for  your buck  and you want to squeeze out that extra 6  tracks in logic pro 10  12 seconds of build time in xcode or 4  seconds for building one-to-one previews  in lightroom classic  then sure get the 10 core but in my  opinion  100 of people should instead spend that  400  on either upgrading the graphics or  something else like the storage  and with that hopefully this video helps  you out and if it did definitely check  out our apple product merch down below  like our mask  and definitely click the circle above  for our upcoming 5k  imac versus 2019 mac pro comparison  video  and for all of you canon eos r5 users  check out max's video right over there  thanks for watching and we'll see you in  the next one

